# Angular 21 Rules

## General

Use modern Angular 21 patterns.

Prefer:

- Standalone components
- Signals when appropriate
- computed()
- modern control flow
- @if
- @for
- @switch
- inject()
- lazy-loaded routes

Avoid legacy Angular patterns when a modern equivalent is appropriate.

## Components

Components should have one clear responsibility.

Avoid components with excessive:
- template size
- business logic
- state
- DOM manipulation

## Inputs and Outputs

Use modern Angular APIs when appropriate.

Prefer clear component contracts.

Do not pass large unrelated objects when only a few fields are required.

## Signals

Use signals for local reactive UI state.

Example:

const isMenuOpen = signal(false);

Use computed() for derived state.

Do not use effect() for ordinary derived values.

## Control Flow

Prefer Angular's modern control flow:

@if
@else
@for
@switch

Avoid introducing unnecessary structural complexity.

## Templates

Keep templates readable.

Avoid large inline expressions.

Move complex logic into:
- computed signals
- component methods when appropriate
- helper functions

## Routing

Use lazy loading for feature routes where beneficial.

Do not lazy-load every tiny component.

## Performance

Avoid:
- unnecessary subscriptions
- repeated expensive calculations
- large initial bundles
- unnecessary third-party libraries

Use:
- lazy loading
- image optimization
- defer loading where appropriate
- semantic HTML

## Angular CLI

Use Angular CLI for generating:

- components
- services
- directives
- pipes
- guards

Do not manually duplicate Angular-generated boilerplate.